Q: Exporting an array in bash script A: <p>I saw a lot of unclear ideas and a lot of text. But I couldn't find an elegant solution.<br />
(Perhaps due to the many replies my post will not be seen.)<br />
1. Environment variables are only visible to child scripts (which are launched from the current script). But child scripts cannot affect parent environment variables in any way.<br />
2. In order for independent scripts to exchange data, a clipboard file is needed.<br />
3. the best import is through the declare and source directives</p>
<p>To write an array to an environment variable<br />
use</p>
<pre><code>export MY_ARR=&quot;$(declare -p my_arr)&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Attention: line breaks are not saved in environment variables. Additional shielding is required.</p>
<p>Example (not been tested)</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>export MY_ARR=&quot;$(echo -e &quot;$(declare -p my_arr)&quot;|sed ':a;N;$!ba;s/\n/\\\\n/g')&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>To import it into the script</p>
<pre><code>
 source &lt;(echo -e &quot;${MY_ARR}&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>To exchange variables between scripts<br />
(Code as an example. May need some modification)</p>
<p>./sare_space.sh</p>
<pre><code>#!/bin/bash

exportVar(){
    _export_var=&quot;$(declare -p $@)&quot;
}
importVar(){
#your data filter
    source &lt;(echo &quot;$@&quot;)
}

exportVarFromDescriptor(){
    &quot;$(declare -p $@)&quot; &gt;&amp;9
}

importVarFromDescriptor(){
    source &lt;(cat &lt;&amp;9)   
}
# Runs the script while creating a clipboard
runScript(){
    #create buffer file
    local temp=$(mktemp -d)
    local buf=&quot;$temp/my_buf&quot;
    echo &quot;$_export_var&quot; &gt; $buf

    #exec 9&lt;&gt;&quot;$pipe&quot;

#Assign a handle to write to the file. This is convenient because you don’t need to tell the script the exchange file.
    exec 9&lt;&gt;&quot;$buf&quot;
# run script
    &quot;$@&quot;
#close descriptor
    exec 9&gt;&amp;-

    importVar &quot;$(cat &quot;$buf&quot;)&quot;

    rm &quot;$buf&quot;
    rm -r $temp
}

</code></pre>
<p>./my_lib.sh</p>
<pre><code>source &quot;./sare_space.sh&quot;
otherLib(){
    exportVar &quot;var_1&quot; &quot;var_2&quot;
    runScript &quot;./otherLib.sh&quot; $@ 
}
otherLib
</code></pre>
<p>./otherLib.sh</p>
<pre><code>source &quot;./sare_space.sh&quot;
importVarFromDescriptor
#... code
exportVarFromDescriptor &quot;var_1&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: Raku solution for discovering words for word puzzle A: <p><strong><em>Final Update</em></strong></p>
<p>Firstly, this is a <em>very nice problem</em>. And it really is simple.<br />
However, no amount of <em>VOODOO</em> can make this any simpler in any language,</p>
<p>This is basically a simple premise that's easily understood.</p>
<p>This approach makes an <em>Intermediate</em> File for each word width.</p>
<p>The example here uses the 250,000 word Dictionary commonly associated with this file:<br />
<code>/usr/share/dict/words</code>.</p>
<p>The Dictionary is read in and partitioned into arrays, each array contain words<br />
that are the distinct number of letters.</p>
<p><strong><em><code>Cross.pl</code></em></strong> This code does the dictionary parsing. It then generates an<br />
intermediate file for each group.</p>
<ul>
<li>The <em><code>Intermediate File</code></em> is a line list of words where each seed word<br />
the left column word. The words to its right differ by one letter from that word.</li>
<li>These Intermediate Files only need to be generated once. They can be re-generated<br />
if there is a new Dictionary that will be used.</li>
<li>They represent a template of a Dictionary.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong><em><code>Cross_Qry.pl</code></em></strong> This code then works with a given input <em>Intermediate File</em><br />
and a given regex <em>filter</em> ex. <code>[rts]</code> to filter a _subset of dif-by-one<br />
lines. Each line found matches and contains a subset that matches the filter.<br />
The result are lines that contain the seed (left word) followed by words that<br />
dif-by-one letter that match the filter regex.</p>
<ul>
<li>The query results are instantaneous. You can do hundreds of query combinations<br />
on a single intermediate file. Or use another intermediate file to query.<br />
Its all instant results.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is list of Intermediate files already generated by <code>Cross.pl</code> using this Dictionary<br />
<a href="https://regexformat.com/dnl/_Samples/_Cross_Misc/250K_Dict.txt" rel="nofollow noreferrer">250K_Dict.txt</a></p>
<p><a href="https://regexformat.com/dnl/_Samples/_Cross_Misc/_03Ltr.txt" rel="nofollow noreferrer">_03Ltr.txt</a>
